  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1033 PM MST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nUpper level high pressure will further build into the area Sunday \nand Monday, keeping well-above normal temperatures and dry \nconditions across the area. Mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies are \nforecast for the next two days, with highs approaching 70 degrees \neach afternoon for portions of the plains. \n\nOn Tuesday, the flow aloft becomes more zonal, and potentially even \na little northwesterly. A shortwave trough is forecast to approach \nthe area from the northwest, bringing slightly cooler temperatures \naloft and increased cloud cover for Tuesday afternoon. This will \nallow temperatures to drop a few degrees for Tuesday afternoon in \nthe higher terrain and portions of the plains. A cold front is \nforecast to move through sometime mid Tuesday into early \nWednesday. Models vary pretty significantly with the positioning \nand strength of the shortwave and therefore the strength of the \ncold front. The GFS wants to bring the shortwave further south \ninto the area, with a much stronger cold front. It also brings the\n700mb jet over the northeast plains, putting breezy winds across \nthe area late Tuesday into Wednesday. This solution would allow \nfor low end rain chances in the higher terrain, warmer \ntemperatures across the northern plains on Tuesday from strong \ndownsloping, and cooler temperatures Wednesday. However, other \nmodels have a less potent shortwave staying more to our northeast.\nWith this solution precipitation chances would remain near zero, \nsurface winds would be much lighter, and temperatures would drop \nslightly for the middle of the week, but not as significantly as \nthe other scenario.\n\nFor Thursday into the weekend, an upper level ridge builds over the \narea, bringing temps back to 10-15 degrees above normal for the \nplains on Friday. Looking at ensembles, there is not a single member \nof the most recent GFS and ECMWF runs that have even a trace of rain \nfor Denver over the next 7 days, so confidence is high in conditions \nstaying dry for the plains (unfortunately).",
